sserts, nothing. OK.\n\nAnother thought: instead of patching builtins.range globally, could I patch it more surgically? There's no way to patch per-module builtins lookup other than the module's globals \u2014 the function's globals are f059's module dict. I could do `monkeypatch.setitem(f059.__dict__, ...)`? No \u2014 range isn't in f059's dict; adding it there would shadow builtins for that module! Actually, that's a cleaner approach: set `f059.range = fake_range` in the module's globals. Since the function looks up `range` in globals first, it would find our fake. And monkeypatch.setitem on the module dict restores it after. This avoids touching global builtins entirely \u2014 much cleaner!\n\n```python\nimport f059\n\ndef test_...(monkeypatch):\n    real_range = range\n    fired = {\"ok\": False}\n    def fake_range(start, stop=None, step=None):\n        if (start, stop) == (2, 2) and step is None and not fired[\"ok\"]:\n            fired[\"ok\"] = True\n            return iter([1])\n        ...\n    monkeypatch.setattr(f059, \"range\", fake_range)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr(f059, \"range\", fake_range) sets f059.range = fake_range (module attribute = module global). The function's global namespace IS f059's module dict, so `range` inside largest_prime_factor resolves to f059.range \u2192 fake. And monkeypatch deletes/restores it after the test. This is cleaner and safer (doesn't affect other modules). \n\nBut one risk: if f059/__init__.py has `from __future__ import ...` or defines `range` \u2014 it doesn't. Also, if the module dict gets restored by deleting the attribute \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr without a prior attribute: by default, `monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value)` will delete the attribute on undo if it didn't exist before? Let me recall: monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value, raising=True) \u2014 on undo, it restores the previous state; if the attribute didn't exist before, it deletes it. Yes, monkeypatch records whether the attribute existed and deletes it on undo if not. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: does setting f059.range affect the `is_prime` inner function's lookup of range? is_prime's globals are also f059's dict. So range(2, k-1) inside is_prime would also use the fake. In our test, is_prime(1) returns before range. Fine.\n\nI'll use the module-attribute patch. Actually, hmm, which is more robust? If the test runner imports f059 differently (e.g., as a top-level module vs package), `import f059` should work either way given it's f059/__init__.py. \n\nBut actually, wait: what if the tests are run with the module imported as `f059` already (conftest adds path)? `import f059` gives the same module object that largest_prime_factor's globals belong to. \u2713.\n\nLet me write both the import and use `f059.largest_prime_factor`.\n\nFinal script:\n\n```python\nimport f059\nfrom f059 import largest_prime_factor\n\n\ndef test_largest_prime_factor_basic_examples():\n    assert largest_prime_factor(15) == 5\n    assert largest_prime_factor(27) == 3\n    assert largest_prime_factor(63) == 7\n    assert largest_prime_factor(13195) == 29\n    assert largest_prime_factor(330) == 11\n\n\ndef test_largest_prime_factor_prime_and_composite_divisors():\n    # n = 4: j=2 -> is_prime(2) (range(2, 1) empty -> True, line 9 via empty loop);\n    #        j=3 -> 4 % 3 != 0, short-circuits before is_prime;\n    #        j=4 -> is_prime(4): 4 % 2 == 0 -> False (line 8).\n    assert largest_prime_factor(4) == 2\n    # n = 9: j=3 -> is_prime(3) (range(2, 2) empty -> True).\n    assert largest_prime_factor(9) == 3\n    assert largest_prime_factor(2) == 2\n    assert largest_prime_factor(3) == 3\n\n\ndef test_largest_prime_factor_loop_skipped_for_n_below_two():\n    # range(2, n + 1) is empty for n <= 1, so the loop body never runs.\n    assert largest_prime_factor(1) == 1\n    assert largest_prime_factor(0) == 1\n\n\ndef test_largest_prime_factor_is_prime_guard_k_below_two(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"The outer loop only ever 